  "query_preview": "..\n(Today through next Thursday)\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW: The main trough to impact our area this\nweek has moved off to the east, however a quick moving weak \ntrough is expected again this evening and overnight. The only \nimpacts expected with this system at this time are a cool night, \nwith lows in the mid 20s, and a few flurries along the Idaho \nborder. Beginning Friday morning a ridge builds over the Southwest\nresulting in extended dry conditions and high temperatures \napproaching ten degrees above normal by Sunday. Daytime highs are \nexpected to be in the low to mid 60s while overnight lows will be \nin the 30s. Winds will remain light during this period, with\nafternoon gusts below 15 mph. Ridging is expected to continue \nthrough at least Wednesday but an atmospheric river is on the \nhorizon. A lot of uncertainty still surrounds this system, \nincluding how much of its moisture will make it to the Great \nBasin. The only thing that can be said with confidence at this \ntime is the AR would break the ridge late next week, which would \nalso decrease temperatures again. The question of how cold and how\nwet will have to wait another few days.\n\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS: High confidence in a weak trough\novernight. High confidence in ridging and the resulting above\naverage temperatures this weekend. Low confidence in the potential\nAR event late next week.",
